*UPD Summary:*

* customer will act as a processor on behalf of its own clients;
* this is normal practice for Bolt Business, and DPA templates were developed previously by the privacy team to cover this privacy arrangement;
* however, upon closer review, the DPA required improvement updated. Main update - removal of the reference to the Older EU Commission SCCs for international transfers (pre-2021)
* updated DPA was shared with the customer on 13th of August. Feedback is pending.

UPD 28.08:

* customer refused the DPA and requested a call last week to discuss their concerns. In short - customer would prefer Bolt to be a data processor for the personal data the customer shares.
* The customer believes that it may share special categories of personal data with Bolt, such as medical institutions names as trip destination (Bolt is used to transfer ill; patients) - via RideBooker API.
* though customer would prefer Bolt to be a data processor, it is not a critical point, and they may agree to Bolt being a data controller if Bolt can undertake to safeguard personal data and not use it for other purposes other than to provide services to the customer;
* this would not affect the personal data that Bolt receives from the customers directly.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Latest UPD (6 September):*

# customer shared its default DPA again, which imposes very strict restrictions at Bolt re/ the processing of personal data under Art. 28 of the GDPR;
# after the review of the DPA, and carefully considering Bolt`s position, it was decided to push back against the DPA again. Reasons:

* *Precedent Concern:* accepting the role of a data processor could set a challenging precedent, as Bolt operates as a data controller in all current arrangements.
* *Compliance Challenges:* complying with all restrictions applicable to data processors would be difficult, as Bolt lacks the necessary procedures for authorising and managing data processors.

# Due to the above, the default DPA was refused and the customer was request to review the DSA template previously shared by Bolt in August.
{panel}

